            }
          },
          {
            "kind": "styled",
            "id": "b010",
            "text": "Variables and modes, one year on",
            "style": {
              "size": 30,
              "weight": "bold"
            }
          },
          {
            "kind": "styled",
            "id": "b011",
            "text": "By Mei-Lin Tsao · 2026-06-11",
            "style": {
              "size": 13,
              "color": "#6b6b6b"
            }
          },
          {
            "kind": "divider",
            "id": "b012"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "b013",
            "text": "When variables shipped, the question we heard most was whether a design tool needed a type system. A year later the answer from the teams we talked to is a clear yes, and mostly for a reason we did not predict: modes made the cost of a theme visible."
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "b014",
            "text": "Before variables, a dark theme was a second copy of every screen. It drifted within a sprint. Now a dark theme is a column in a collection, and the diff between light and dark is a list you can read in one sitting. Teams told us they were finally able to delete the second copy."
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "b015",
            "text": "The other shift was the export path. Typed collections can be exported as tokens JSON, which means the same file that designers edit is the source the build reads. The Atlas Design System on the community is a good example: the variables in the file are the variables in the CSS."
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "b016",
            "text": "What is next: expressions in prototypes can now read and write variables, and Grid auto layout uses variables for track sizes. We are working on collection-level permissions so a platform team can own the primitives while product teams own their aliases."
          },
          {
